---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Research Agenda"
  description="Current and past projects, and interdisciplinary ideas of Filippo Carmenati."
  bodyClass="research-agenda"
>
  <style is:global>
    main .ra-heading { font-size: 36px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
    main .ra-body h2 {
      font-size: 24px;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin: 2.25rem 0 1rem;
    }
    main .ra-body h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
    main .ra-body h3 {
      font-size: 19px;
      font-weight: 700;
      margin: 1.75rem 0 0.75rem;
    }
    main .ra-body p,
    main .ra-body ul {
      font-size: 17px;
      line-height: 1.6;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }
    main .ra-body ul {
      padding-left: 1.25rem;
    }
    main .ra-body li {
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }
    main .ra-body hr {
      margin: 2.5rem 0;
      border: none;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    }
  </style>

  <h1 class="ra-heading">Research Agenda</h1>

  <div class="ra-body">
    <h2>Projects</h2>

    <h3>1. CROPDIVA (<a href="https://cropdiva.eu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://cropdiva.eu</a>) (2021&ndash;2026)</h3>

    <p>
      CROPDIVA concentrated on six underutilised or &ldquo;orphan&rdquo; crops (Oat, Hull-less barley, Narrow-leaved lupin, buckwheat, Tritricale, Faba bean) because diversifying what we grow is essential for a resilient and healthy food system. This has been the project I worked on at Agroscope throughout my PhD.
    </p>

    <p>The tasks done consisted in:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Supervising of a 3-year mixed cropping experiment (lentil &amp; hull-less barley) across two Swiss sites, managing full experimental cycles from plot establishment to harvest and post-harvest analysis (laboratory analysis of grain quality parameters - beta-glucan content, mycotoxin content - and grain separation performance).</li>
      <li>Conducted agronomic and morphological field observations of a spring and winter barley diversity panel of 300+ genotypes, complemented by image-based canopy cover analysis (Canopeo) and post-harvest NIR spectroscopy.</li>
      <li>Quantified provisioning and regulating ecosystem services (pollination, weed suppression, yield stability) delivered by four underutilized crops under mono- and mixed cropping systems.</li>
      <li>Producing manuscripts and presenting results at national and international conferences.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Keywords: <strong>Niche crops, intercropping, protein crops, diversifications</strong></p>

    <hr />

    <h2>Interdisciplinary ideas:</h2>

    <p>I like mixing not only plants but also different disciplines.</p>

    <p>Following the PhD, I started writing projects for which I am looking for collaborators:</p>

    <h3>1. &ldquo;Mix them up!: a card game&rdquo;</h3>

    <p>
      The night after submitting my PhD thesis, a flash of inspiration got me. I decided to condense what learnt from my PhD into a card game that could be used in classes for teaching the effect of crop mixtures along spatial and temporal scale.
    </p>

    <p>Keywords: <strong>Education, gamification, agroecology, game design</strong></p>

    <h3>2. &ldquo;IC2field&rdquo;</h3>

    <p>
      Intercropping experiments often lack a systemic vision that addresses not only agronomic and ecological aspects, but also <strong>gene expression, soil microbiology, pedoclimatic conditions, computational analysis for crop model calibration, and, most importantly, farmers</strong>. How can all these aspects be connected harmoniously?
    </p>

    <p>
      I am exploring how intercropping experiments could be approached from a more <strong>systemic perspective</strong>, connecting processes and disciplines that are often investigated separately.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Can we develop better ways of understanding the complexity of diversified cropping systems?</strong></p>

    <p>Keywords: <strong>Transcriptomic, agroecology, soil microbiology, crop modelling</strong></p>

    <h3>3. &ldquo;Social pace and biological pace&rdquo;</h3>

    <p>
      Technology is accelerating almost everything around us: communication, information, transportation, entertainment, consumption, and even the sensory environments we inhabit. This acceleration has become so ordinary that we rarely notice it.
    </p>

    <p>But our biology has not been designed at technological speed.</p>

    <p><strong>What happens when the environment changes faster than the organism?</strong></p>

    <p>Keywords: <strong>Social acceleration · Biological rhythms · Human adaptation</strong></p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
